<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">García Sánchez I of Pamplona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 919–970</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">García Sánchez I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> from 925 to 970, for many years under the influence of his mother Queen Toda. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andregoto-galindez">Andregoto Galíndez</a> brought the County of Aragon under the crown of Pamplona. He was succeeded by his son Sancho II Garcés, who gave the Rioja lands of Viguera to his brother Ramiro.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>García Sánchez I, son of Sancho I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona">Toda Aznárez</a>, was born around 919 and succeeded his father in 925 at the age of six, the second king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimenez-dynasty">Jiménez dynasty</a>. He reigned at first under the tutelage of his uncle Jimeno Garcés and his mother Toda. Three of his sisters married kings of León: Urraca married Ramiro II, Oneca married Alfonso IV, and Sancha Sánchez married first Ordoño II and later Fernán González, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><p>Another sister, Velasquita, married Munio Vélaz, a count of Álava. The historian Gonzalo Martínez Díez says these family ties meant that soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> often fought beside Leonese and Castilian troops against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Queen Toda's web of alliances raised the kingdom's power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Jimeno Garcés died on 29 May 931, another uncle, Íñigo Garcés, probably tried to become regent and may have tried to take the throne. In May 934 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, Abd-ar-Rahman III, marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II of León</a> and was about to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> when Toda sent an embassy asking him to withdraw. He made her come to his camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calahorra</a>, where she accepted a pact of submission and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, and in return he invested García with Pamplona and its districts.</p><p>In 937 García allied himself with Ramiro II and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Muhammad ibn Hashim, provoking a caliphal campaign into his lands. In July 939 he fought alongside Ramiro II and Fernán González at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a>, where the Christian armies defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s forces.</p><p>After the death of Ramiro II and of Ordoño III of León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-i-of-leon">Sancho I of León</a>, García's nephew. When Fernán González installed his own son-in-law Ordoño IV in Sancho's place, relations broke down, and García intervened in León, captured Fernán and restored Sancho. Fernán made territorial concessions to win his release, and the alliance was restored in 964, when he married García's daughter Urraca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>García married his first cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andregoto-galindez">Andregoto Galíndez</a>, daughter of Galindo Aznárez II, Count of Aragon. As Galindo had no legitimate male heirs, the rights to Aragon passed to Andregoto and then to her first son, as a county within the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. He repudiated her in 942 and by 943 had married Teresa Ramírez, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II of León</a>. His son by Andregoto, Sancho II, succeeded him. A son by Teresa, Ramiro Garcés, became the first King of Viguera. García died on 22 February 970 and was buried in the castle of San Esteban de Deyo at Villamayor de Monjardín.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “García Sánchez I of Pamplona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its native counts of the Galindo family kept close ties with Pamplona, and in the early 10th century the heiress Andregoto Galíndez married <b>García Sánchez I of Pamplona</b>, bringing the county under the Navarrese crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-aragon">County of Aragon</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 970 King Sancho II Garcés of Pamplona, following the will of his father <b>García Sánchez I</b>, granted the Rioja lands around Viguera to his younger brother Ramiro Garcés with the title of king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-viguera">Kingdom of Viguera</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Pamplona. Died in Navarre, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-1.65&amp;year=952" aria-label="Navarre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4510 643 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of García Sánchez I of Pamplona"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4960 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4960 913h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-1.65&amp;year=952">What was here in 952 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
